        "h": "4. Wyoming: The Gold Standard for Non-Resident E-Commerce and Privacy",
        "body": "<p style=\"margin-bottom: 14px; line-height: 1.75;\">Wyoming holds a revered historical status: it is the birthplace of the American Limited Liability Company, having enacted the nation's inaugural LLC statute in 1977. For non-resident digital entrepreneurs, Wyoming remains the undisputed market leader for four distinct statutory reasons:</p>\n<ul style=\"margin: 16px 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;\">\n  <li style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>Strict Member Anonymity:</strong> Wyoming law does not require the names of LLC members (owners) or managers to be listed in the public Articles of Organization or the annual report. Only the registered agent's name and address appear on the Secretary of State's database. This creates an impenetrable legal barrier against scraping bots, aggressive competitors, and public surveillance.</li>\n  <li style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>Zero Corporate & Personal Income Tax:</strong> The state imposes no corporate income tax, no personal state income tax, no capital gains tax, and no gross receipts franchise tax. If your business operates digitally, your state tax bill in Wyoming is permanently zero.</li>\n  <li style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>Low Recurring Compliance Overhead:</strong> Wyoming mandates an annual report fee of just $62 (for entities with assets under $300,000 located in Wyoming). Unlike Delaware's $300 tax or Nevada's $350 annual license toll, Wyoming respects the cash flow of early-stage digital operators.</li>\n  <li style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>Exclusive Charging Order Protection:</strong> Under Wyoming Statute § 17-29-503, a 'charging order' is the exclusive legal remedy by which a judgment creditor of a member can satisfy a judgment out of the member's distributional interest. The creditor cannot seize the LLC's assets, cannot force a liquidation of company property, and cannot participate in company management. This statutory protection applies equally to single-member LLCs, shielding your commercial assets completely.</li>\n</ul>"
